{
  "dom_id": "step-q353254",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The growth rate of the number of students enrolled in an after-school program, $P(t),$ is jointly proportional to the number of students enrolled at time $t,$ measured in weeks, and the difference between the program\u2019s maximum enrollment and the number of students enrolled.\n\nGiven that the constant of proportionality is $0.004$ and the maximum enrollment is $500,$ find an expression for the number of students enrolled after $t$ weeks, given that there were $50$ students enrolled initially.\n\n| $P(t)\\,=\\,\\,$ | $500$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $1\\,+\\,$ {{field-1}} |  |",
  "worked_solution": "Since the number of students enrolled grows at a rate that is *jointly* proportional to the number of students enrolled at time $t$ and the difference between the maximum enrollment and the number of students enrolled, the number of students enrolled follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.004,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=500.$ Therefore, the growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.004\\,P(500-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{500}{1+C{e}^{-(500\\cdot 0.004)t}}=\\frac{500}{1+C{e}^{-2t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=50$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{500}{1+C{e}^{-2\\cdot 0}} & =50 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{500}{50} \\\\ C & =10-1 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{500}{1+9{e}^{-2t}}.\n$$",
